across the valley agencies are teaming  up to provide domestic abuse training  mary jane belliesa shares how safe nest  is helping first responders identify and  help those in need  january is human trafficking prevention  month and the las vegas fire department  and safeness are partnering together to  provide first responder training on how  to support victims of domestic violence  and how to spot human trafficking dina  d'alessio deputy chief of operations for  las vegas fire and rescue says this will  be a yearly training as firefighters  learn how to recognize the signs the  misconceptions of human trafficking and  much more some of the images that we're  shown actually are very impactful for us  i mean it usually will show branding  the interaction that the firefighters  will have with that person would usually  shows that they're withdrawn they don't  have a phone they seem to be fearful of  the person who's probably 30 feet away  safeness ceo liz ortenberger says  through these awareness trainings first  responders can immediately provide  domestic violence survivors with  resources and shelter we're the third  most dangerous place in the country for  women being murdered by men before the  pandemic but here's the win  we will be  lower in that ranking as we come out and  in a better spot because of our  partnerships with both fire and las  vegas metro while we have seen an  increase in domestic violence incidences  we have not seen the correlating  increase in homicides and that's  directly related to partnerships like  this so the community can help by i mean  there are eyes and ears anytime that  they see something say something  obviously call 911  and will be there to respond reporting  from the las vegas fire and rescue  center mary jane ballieza live local now
 

